\begin_layout Part*
Overview of the algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are the main steps of the phase mask finder walker algorithm, using
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

AND
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in our deltas:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We create a vector of possible deltas to change the phase mask gradient.
 This must include the value 0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We find the locations on the phase mask which are of interest to us.
 This means the locations that LF camera intersects with, using the angles
 and locations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We interpolate the values of the phase mask gradient in these locations,
 based on the phase mask gradient from the previous iteration (initialized
 as 0s) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We find the FW locations of the mask, for each possible delta
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We interpolate the mask at these locations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We multiply with the LF, to get scores for each delta
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We create a transformation matrix, to convert from scores on the LF space,
 to scores in the phase mask space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We convert the scores to the phase mask space 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We take the best delta for each phase mask location, based on the scores
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We pass this delta map through a LPF 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We create a vector of possible step sizes to change the phase mask gradient.
 This must include the value 0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We repeat step 2 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We repeat step 3, based on an updated phase mask gradient, using a specific
 step size 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We find the FW locations of the mask 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We interpolate the mask at these locations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We multiply with the LF, and sum to get the score for a specific step size
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We take the best step size, and update the phase mask gradient 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to help the explanation be clear, we will define several things.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
LF
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, a lightfield LF has 4 variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X,Y,k_{x},k_{y}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is constant, we can define 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{x}=\sin\theta_{x},k_{x}=\sin\theta_{y}$
\end_inset

, ignoring the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 angle is defind between the ray (after projecting to 2d slice) and the
 z axis (the optical axis).
 A light ray coming from the right (going left) will have a positive 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 This is how it seems to be defined in the LF object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, a lightfield space has 4 variables...
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distance between the LF and the phase mask is 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Phase Mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Phase mask can be seen as a 2D function that has 2 variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X,Y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function returns a change in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k_{x},\Delta k_{y}$
\end_inset

.We will notate this function as 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\phi}\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We will define the change as thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a ray passes the Phase Mask with momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{k}$
\end_inset

 the output ray will have momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{k}+\Delta\vec{k}$
\end_inset

 before normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3 Different LFs Spaces
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have 3 different LF spaces.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, the one that was input.
 We will notate this by the letter 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Second, we have the LF we will reconstruct, which will be notated by the
 letter 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have the Amplitude mask.
 In this case we will use the letter 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us go over the algorithm steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Algorithm Steps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We create a deltas array 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta k$
\end_inset

.
 In our case, we are tying both axes, so each element in the array is actually
 a 2d vector in itself: 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta k_{x},\delta k_{y}$
\end_inset

.
 We will go over each of these possible delta vectors.
 We include the value 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(0,0\right)$
\end_inset

 so that we can always compare to the previous iteration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each location on 
\begin_inset Formula $LF$
\end_inset

 (for now it doesn't matter which LF, as they have the same axes) we find
 where the ray intersects with the phase mask.
 At location 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $LF$
\end_inset

 the intersecting point will be:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(x-\Delta x,y-\Delta y\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x=-L\tan\theta_{x}=-L\frac{\sin\theta_{x}}{\cos\theta_{x}}=-L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{PM}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)=x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{PM}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)=y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we interpolate the change in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 due to the phase mask at this location:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\Delta k}=\vec{\phi}\left(x_{PM},y_{PM}\right)=\vec{\phi}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we find the FW location for each LF location.
 We do this by adding a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta k$
\end_inset

 to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k$
\end_inset

 that we have found in 3, and so find the FW location.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, the new location is at:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{FW}=x-\Delta x+\Delta x_{new}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x=-L\tan\theta_{x}=-L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x_{new}=-L\cdot\tan\theta_{x,new}=-L\cdot\frac{k_{x,new}}{k_{z,new}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{x,new}=k_{x}-\Delta k_{x}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{y,new}=k_{y}-\Delta k_{y}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{z,new}=k\cdot\left(1-k_{x,new}^{2}-k_{y,new}^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All in all:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{FW}=x+L\left(\cdot\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}-\frac{k_{x,new}}{k_{z,new}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{FW}=y+L\left(\cdot\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}-\frac{k_{y,new}}{k_{z,new}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we interpolate the Amplitude Mask at these locations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LF_{m}\left(x_{FW},y_{FW}\right)=LF_{m}\left(x_{FW},y_{FW}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The angle here doesn't matter, as this is the same for all angles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We multiply with the 
\begin_inset Formula $LF_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for each point in 
\begin_inset Formula $LF_{i}$
\end_inset

 we get a certain score:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LF_{i}\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)\cdot LF_{m}\left(x_{FW},y_{FW},\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where of course 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{FW},y_{FW}$
\end_inset

 are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
7.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have now found, for each delta, the score that this delta has for a certain
 location in the phase mask.
 We have already found this location in step 2.
 However, this score is now in the LF space.
 |We want it to be in the Phase Mask space.
 Thus, we create a transformation mask.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each point in LF space we have found in the interpolation can be defined
 as a point relevant to 4 points in the phase mask space grid.
 We flatten the Score/Weight matrix (what we got in step 6), to get 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{LF}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to convert this to 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{PM}$
\end_inset

(PM for phase mask).
 Thus, we need a transformation matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{PM}=M\cdot W_{LF}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice the sizes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(W_{LF}\right)_{\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(W_{PM}\right)_{\left|X_{PM}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{PM}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M_{\left|X_{PM}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{PM}\right|,\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to create 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 we do the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find for each point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{PM},y_{PM}\right)$
\end_inset

 the 4 relevant indices in the Phase Mask grid they fall in between.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Test if these indices are valid, as in, are they in the grid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the weights for each of these 4 pixels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Convert these indices to indices for the flattened Phase Mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Define the relevant rows in the 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 The relevant rows are the relevant indices we have found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Define the relevant columns, which just make sure that we take each point
 4, as mentioned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Create a Sparse matrix, with these rows, columns, and weights as defined
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The difficult part here is making sure we are doing the flattening right,
 and taking it into account correctly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we can convert the Weights/Score:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{PM}=M\cdot W_{LF}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
9.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{LF}$
\end_inset

 is actually a 5D matrix, the first 4 are of the LF, but the last one is
 of the deltas 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\delta k}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for each delta, we get a different Score map in the Phase Mask Space.
 For each location in this space, we choose the best one.
 As in:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\delta k}\left(x,y\right)=\underset{\vec{\delta k}}{\text{argmax}}W_{PM}\left(x,y\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
10.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We got a delta map 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\delta k}\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We pass this through a LPF
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We create a possible vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

's.
 This must include 0, and preferably 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We repeat step 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We repeat step 3, but now with an updated Phase Mask, like so:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\Delta k}=\vec{\phi}_{new}\left(x_{PM},y_{PM}\right)=\vec{\phi}_{new}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\phi}_{new}\left(x,y\right)=\vec{\phi}\left(x,y\right)+\alpha\cdot\vec{\delta k}\left(x,y\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
14.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We repeat step 4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We repeat step 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
16.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We repeat step 6 to get:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{LF}\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)=LF_{i}\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)\cdot LF_{m}\left(x_{FW},y_{FW},\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And now we sum 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{LF}$
\end_inset

 up to get a total score for a specific 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We choose the best alpha, as in:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\alpha}=\underset{\alpha}{\text{argmax}}\text{Sum}\left(W_{LF}\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y},\alpha\right)\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\underset{\alpha}{\text{argmax}}\text{Sum}\left(LF_{i}\left(x,y,\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)\cdot LF_{m}\left(x_{FW}\left(\alpha\right),y_{FW}\left(\alpha\right),\theta_{x},\theta_{y}\right)\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And then we update the Phase Mask:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\phi}\left(x,y\right)=\vec{\phi}\left(x,y\right)+\hat{\alpha}\cdot\vec{\delta k}\left(x,y\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

